In article <Cn72q3.5pE@seagoon.newcastle.edu.au>, c9317103@alinga.newcastle.edu.au (Andrew Hardy) writes:
>The ideal armor/rings combination (as far as I am concerned) is:-
>
>blessed fireproof hawaiian shirt
>blessed rustproof helm of brilliance    (increase Wi and In)
>blessed rustproof shield of reflection	(gain reflection intr.)
         ********* Sheilds of reflection are already rustproof.
>blessed rustproof gray dragon scale mail (gain magic-prot. intr.)
         ********* GDSM is already rustproof
>blesesd rustproof cloak of displacement  (gain displacement intr.)
>blessed rustproof gloves of power        (increase St)
>blessed rustproof speed boots            (increase speed)
>

A cloak of displacement + GDSM is one of the best combinations there is.
One small problem with it is that a cloak of displacement only has a 
cancellation of 2.  The following cloaks offer a cancellation of 3
(much better protection against level drains and other magic attacks):
  - elven cloak
  - oilskin cloak
  - cloak of protection
  - cloak of magic resistance

As far as cancellation goes, BDSM + a cloak of magic resistance is much 
better.  For neutral characters who can't get complete protection from
level drains from Excalibur/Stormbringer, this might be a better 
combination in certain circumstances (eg: when entering a big wraith
filled graveyard, when going on the priest quest, ...).

>ring of +? protection			(gain protection intr.)
>
>NOTE: Unless your god decides to protect you get the ring.
This is really only additional AC.  I prefer to have the rings
with more interesting magic effects.  I don't always wear them, 
but I like to keep them handy:
  - conflict
  - polymorph control
  - polymorph
  - regeneration (at early levels).
>
>ideal food:
>
>Any color dragon meat tinned or not tinned (preferably tinned) except 
>gray dragon will gain you the relevant resistance intrinsics.
>
Untinned dragon meat is way too heavy to lug around.  Tinned food has
very little nutritional value (It's only good for the intrinsics).
I think lembas wafers have the best weight/nutrition ratio (I haven't
checked recently).  Tinned floating eye corpses are handy to keep 
around in case you lose your intrinsic telepathy.  Lizard corpses are
handy (unless you genocide cockatrices).  Royal Jelly is quite yummy, 
and wraith corpses are great (but not very filling).  I'm not sure that
there is one ideal food it depends on your situation.  Certainly dragon
corpses are not great once you have the intrinsics they give.

